DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Lukács's final, unfinished project, Zur Ontologie des gesellschaftlichen Seins
(written 1960s, published posthumously from 1971), attempts to ground Marxism
in a general ontology of "social being" built outward from labor as the
"model" (Modell) of all teleological positing (teleologische Setzung).
Where History and Class Consciousness (HCC) had located the resolution of the
subject-object antinomy in the revolutionary self-consciousness of the
proletariat, the Ontology relocates the analysis at a more basic level: every
act of labor posits an end (teleology) that is realized only through
manipulation of an objectively existing causal nexus (causality), and it is
this labor-model, generalized and made "more social" (gesellschaftlicher) at
successively higher levels of complexity, that is supposed to explain the
genesis of value, ideology, alienation, and historical development without
recourse to a metaphysical world-spirit or a single collective revolutionary
subject-object. The reception history of this text is unusually contested.
The "Budapest School" -- Lukács's own students Fehér, Heller, Márkus, and
Vajda -- published a scathing internal critique in 1976 charging that the
manuscript oscillates between two incompatible ontologies (a dogmatic,
quasi-Engelsian "ontology of nature" and a genuinely dialectical "ontology of
social being"), a critique that, per Forczek's 2024 rebuttal captured in
Domain 1 of this review, has disproportionately shaped (and arguably
suppressed) the Ontology's later reception. Nicolas Tertulian, by contrast,
across several decades of essays, treats the Ontology as Lukács's most
original and underappreciated achievement, particularly in confrontation with
Heidegger and Nicolai Hartmann. A separate, largely English-language strand
(Joós, Browne, Duayer and Medeiros, Thompson's edited volume, Dorahy) asks
whether the Ontology's categorial apparatus is best read as a fulfillment,
correction, or quiet abandonment of the identical-subject-object claims of
HCC -- a debate this domain surveys directly, in contrast to Domain 1's
broader periodization question and Domain 3's treatment of totality and
subject-object in HCC itself.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the endpoint of the continuity-vs-rupture debate that
runs throughout the review: the Ontology's labor-teleology model is Lukács's
own final verdict on the philosophy-of-history problems posed by HCC, so any
overall assessment of his constructive theory of history must take a position
on how (or whether) the late work resolves, revises, or evades the
subject-object and teleology problems left open in the 1920s.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
No complete English translation of the Prolegomena zur Ontologie des
gesellschaftlichen Seins (the introductory volume, distinct from the three
translated substantive volumes on Hegel, Marx, and Labour) could be verified
via the search scripts, so it is not included as a primary source here;
English-language treatments of the Prolegomena specifically (as opposed to
the translated volumes) remain thin. Guido Oldrini's extensive Italian-language
work on Lukács was searched but yielded no items specifically addressing the
late Ontology (his located essays concern the young Lukács and the Second
International); this appears to be a genuine gap in the English/searchable
literature rather than an oversight. The "post festum-rationality" debate
(Forczek 2024) and the Ninos 2025 Hegel-Logic critique are treated in Domains
1 and 4 respectively and are not duplicated here, though both bear directly
on this domain's questions.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around three axes: (1) primary-text description of the
labor/teleology/causality model itself; (2) the Budapest School rupture
charge and its rebuttals (Tosel, Duayer and Medeiros, Dorahy); (3) applications
and extensions of the ontology beyond exegesis (Fuchs on digital labor,
Blanton on judgment, Vaisman and Fortes on late politics). Cross-reference
Domain 1's Forczek entry and Domain 4's Ninos entry when discussing the
Budapest School critique and the Hegel-Logic lineage of teleological positing.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Rupture/incompatibility (Budapest School): 1 paper (Fehér, Heller, Márkus,
  Vajda 1976) - the Ontology mixes two incompatible ontologies and regresses
  toward Soviet-style dialectical materialism.
- Fulfillment/deepening (Tertulian, Duayer and Medeiros, Tosel, Browne): 5
  papers - the Ontology is Lukács's most mature and philosophically serious
  achievement, continuous with but superior to HCC's unresolved antinomies.
- Applied/extension readings (Fuchs, Blanton, Vaisman and Fortes, Correa
  Castañeda): 4 papers - treat the categorial apparatus (teleological
  positing, labor-model) as a still-usable analytic tool independent of the
  continuity debate.
====================================================================
}

@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Frankfurt School, Western Marxism, and Later Reception
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-07-17
PAPERS_FOUND: 22 total (High: 14, Medium: 8, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain traces how Lukács's philosophy of history was inherited, transformed, and
contested outside his own corpus, beginning with the Frankfurt School. Adorno's Negative
Dialectics and the closely related History and Freedom lecture course retain Lukács's
diagnosis of reification as the structuring pathology of bourgeois society but reject
the "identical subject-object" solution: where Lukács locates a standpoint (the
proletariat) from which reified appearance and historical essence coincide, Adorno's
non-identity thinking treats any claimed identity of subject and object as itself a form
of domination, so that reification becomes permanent and totality becomes purely
negative (a diagnostic category, never an achieved reconciliation). Bueno's 2024 paper
and the recent Kavoulakos/Feenberg/Honneth literature reconstruct this relationship in
detail, showing both continuity (reification as the master category of capitalist
unfreedom) and rupture (the abandonment of a revolutionary-subject solution). Honneth's
Reification: A New Look at an Old Idea (the volume anchored by his 2006 Tanner Lectures)
represents the next major transformation: he "de-ontologizes" reification, reading it
through a recognition-theoretic lens as the forgetting of an original, existential
recognition rather than as a totalizing feature of capitalist objectivity, provoking
sharp responses (Foster, Kavoulakos) that a properly Lukácsian/Frankfurt-School reading
of reification cannot survive this move.

The domain also situates Lukács within "Western Marxism" alongside Korsch and Gramsci,
the trio canonically grouped together (by Merleau-Ponty, Perry Anderson, and others) as
founders of a non-orthodox, philosophically self-conscious Marxism that reasserted the
Hegelian dialectic and the theory-practice unity against Second International
scientism. Korsch's Marxism and Philosophy (1923) makes the historicist case, parallel
to Lukács, that Marxist theory is itself a historically specific moment of the class
struggle rather than a value-neutral science; Devlin's and Skidmore-Hess's recent essays
recover the specificity of Korsch's position and its interwar reception. Gramsci's
Prison Notebooks, composed independently of and largely without access to History and
Class Consciousness, develop cognate but distinct concepts (hegemony, the philosophy of
praxis, the organic intellectual) that later comparativists use to test how far "Western
Marxism" names a single position versus a family resemblance.

Finally, the domain covers the diffusion and contestation of Lukács's philosophy of
history in later Marxist theory: Goldmann's Lukács and Heidegger reconstructs Lukács as
uncredited source for existentialist ontology while marking his own departures; Jameson's
career-long engagement (from Marxism and Form through The Antinomies of Realism)
redeploys totality and reification as categories of literary-historical interpretation,
increasingly in tension with poststructuralist and postmodern critiques of totalizing
thought; and Postone's Time, Labor, and Social Domination mounts the most thorough
critique, arguing that Lukács (like all "traditional Marxism") mistakes capitalism's
historically specific abstract domination for a transhistorical struggle between labor
and its alienated products, and so cannot ground a coherent critique of value and
abstract labor themselves. Devyver's 2025 essay and Jay's 2020 memorial essay show this
Postone-Adorno-Lukács triangulation remains a live site of debate in contemporary
critical theory.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the primary "afterlife" material the project needs to show how
Lukács's constructive philosophy of history (subject-object identity, totality, the
proletarian standpoint) was both extended and dismantled by his most influential
successors, and it supplies the comparative material (Korsch, Gramsci) needed to specify
what, if anything, is distinctive about Lukács's own position within Western Marxism as
a whole.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Direct textual engagement between Walter Benjamin and Lukács is thin in the indexed
secondary literature (most treatments bundle Benjamin into general "Frankfurt School"
surveys rather than analyzing a specific Benjamin-Lukács relation); a dedicated
comparative treatment of Horkheimer's early 1930s materialism against Lukács's 1923
position is similarly underrepresented in the searchable literature. Coverage of
Gramsci's own texts is necessarily limited to the standard English selection rather than
the complete critical edition. Thirteen entries are marked INCOMPLETE/no-abstract:
most are canonical books (Adorno, Korsch, Gramsci, Honneth, Goldmann, Postone, Jameson x2,
Homer) for which no API (Semantic Scholar/OpenAlex/CORE) returns a genuine content
abstract, plus two book chapters (Kavoulakos) and one very recent 2025 article (Devyver)
not yet indexed with an abstract; NDPR fallback searches for these titles returned
mismatched reviews of unrelated books and were deliberately discarded rather than
included, per this project's citation-integrity requirements. These entries are excluded
from automated synthesis but their CORE ARGUMENT/RELEVANCE/POSITION notes below are
based on direct reading of search-result content, not on the (removed) abstracts.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around three axes: (1) inheritance-with-rupture (Adorno/Honneth vis-à-vis
reification and subject-object identity), (2) comparative Western Marxism (Lukács,
Korsch, Gramsci as a triangulated founding moment), and (3) later reconstruction/critique
(Goldmann, Jameson, Postone), using Feenberg and Kavoulakos as recurring cross-referents
who explicitly stage the Frankfurt-School/Lukács relationship.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Negative-dialectical inheritance: 3 papers (Adorno's two primary texts, Bueno) -
  reification retained, subject-object identity rejected as itself reifying.
- Recognition-theoretic transformation: 4 papers (Honneth's primary text and critics
  Foster, Kavoulakos x2) - reification reread as forgetfulness of recognition, decoupled
  from capitalism as such.
- Western Marxism comparativism: 4 papers (Korsch and Gramsci primary sources, Devlin,
  Skidmore-Hess) - tests unity/diversity of the "Western Marxist" founding generation.
- Later reconstruction and critique: 9 papers (Goldmann + Lotz, Jameson x2 + Schubertová +
  Homer, Postone x2 + Jay + Devyver) - ranges from existentialist appropriation to full
  rejection of the labor-based philosophy of history as "traditional Marxism."
====================================================================
}

@misc{stahl2013lukacs,
  author = {Stahl, Titus},
  title = {Georg [György] Lukács},
  year = {2013},
  howpublished = {\url{https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lukacs/}},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This Stanford Encyclopedia entry provides the standard reference biography and philosophical overview of Lukács, narrating the trajectory from the Budapest and Heidelberg years (Soul and Form, Theory of the Novel, the unpublished "Heidelberg Aesthetics") through the 1918 Communist Party membership, the 1919 Hungarian Soviet Republic, History and Class Consciousness (1923), the Moscow exile and public retraction of HCC (1933), to the late Aesthetics and Ontology of Social Being. It flags that the degree of Lukács's genuine agreement with Stalinism "is disputed to this day," citing Lichtheim, Deutscher, Kolakowski, and Pike as representing different positions.

  RELEVANCE: Functions as the master timeline and bibliographic index against which all periodization arguments in this domain are checked; its citation of unresolved disputes (Stalinism, sincerity of self-criticism) is itself evidence that periodization remains a live interpretive problem rather than a settled biographical fact.

  POSITION: Reference/overview; implicitly favors a continuity reading by tracing single concepts (totality, reification, anti-positivism) across periods without asserting an epistemological break.
  },
  keywords = {periodization, biography, encyclopedia, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{lowy2023lukacs,
  author = {Löwy, Michael},
  title = {Georg Lukács: From Romanticism to Bolshevism},
  year = {2023},
  publisher = {Verso},
  address = {New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Löwy argues that Lukács's turn to Bolshevism was not an abandonment of his pre-Marxist "romantic anti-capitalist" sensibility but its dialectical continuation: the same revulsion against the "disenchantment" and reification of bourgeois modernity that animated Soul and Form and The Theory of the Novel is redirected, after 1917-18, toward revolutionary politics rather than aesthetic or ethical withdrawal. Löwy reconstructs this shift through close reading of Lukács's Sunday Circle milieu, his Weberian and neo-Kantian formation, and the specific political conjuncture of the Hungarian Soviet Republic.

  RELEVANCE: Supplies the paradigm case for the "continuity-of-problem" position in the periodization debate: the pre-Marxist and Marxist Lukács are read as successive articulations of one underlying diagnosis (loss of totality/meaning under modernity), directly bearing on how the review should treat the relation between Theory of the Novel and the historical-materialist theory of history in HCC.

  POSITION: Continuity model ("romantic anti-capitalism" evolving into revolutionary Marxism); explicitly opposed to readings that treat 1918 as an epistemological break. First published 1979 (New Left Books); cited here in the 2023 Verso reissue, translated/edited by Patrick Camiller.
  },
  keywords = {periodization, romanticism, Bolshevism, continuity-thesis, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{aratobreines1979younglukacs,
  author = {Arato, Andrew and Breines, Paul},
  title = {The Young Lukács and the Origins of Western Marxism},
  year = {1979},
  publisher = {Pluto Press},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Arato and Breines argue that Western Marxism as a distinct tradition (as opposed to Second- and Third-International orthodoxy) originates in problems Lukács inherited from his pre-Marxist engagement with Simmel, Weber, and German Idealism -- above all the problem of "reification" understood as a successor category to the earlier aesthetic-existential problem of "soul and form." They present History and Class Consciousness as the point where these pre-Marxist categories are transposed onto the standpoint of the proletariat.

  RELEVANCE: Alongside Löwy, this is the founding statement of the continuity thesis and the source of the phrase "the young Lukács" as an analytic category; it directly shapes how commentators periodize the relationship between Theory of the Novel's philosophy of history and the mature theory of reification and totality.

  POSITION: Continuity model; credited (with Löwy) in later scholarship as having established "the Lukács question" as a genuine problem in the historiography of Western Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {periodization, Western-Marxism, continuity-thesis, reification, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{congdon1983younglukacs,
  author = {Congdon, Lee},
  title = {The Young Lukács},
  year = {1983},
  publisher = {University of North Carolina Press},
  address = {Chapel Hill},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Drawing on manuscripts and correspondence discovered in the Lukács Archive in 1972, Congdon presents the first archivally grounded intellectual biography of Lukács's pre-1918 years, arguing that his understanding of Simmel, Dostoevsky, and Hegel was shaped as much by personal crisis -- his relationships with Irma Seidler, Jelena Grabenko, and Gertrud Bortstieber -- as by abstract philosophical argument, and that the mature Lukács's trajectory "from unrestricted freedom to unrestricted despotism" reflects a personality that "hated injustice more than he loved human beings."

  RELEVANCE: Offers a biographically contingent counterweight to Löwy's and Arato/Breines's more philosophically deterministic continuity narratives; useful for testing whether the periodization of Lukács's thought should be explained by internal conceptual logic or external biographical circumstance.

  POSITION: Biographical/contingency model, skeptical of strong continuity or teleological readings of the pre-Marxist-to-Marxist transition.
  },
  keywords = {periodization, biography, archive, young-Lukacs, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{gluck1985lukacsgeneration,
  author = {Gluck, Mary},
  title = {Georg Lukács and His Generation, 1900-1918},
  year = {1985},
  publisher = {Harvard University Press},
  address = {Cambridge, MA},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Gluck situates the young Lukács within the "Sunday Circle" network of Budapest intellectuals (Karl Mannheim, Arnold Hauser, Béla Balázs, Anna Lesznai) and argues that revolutionary socialism was not the inevitable outcome of his early cultural radicalism but only one of several live options within the fragmented ideological climate of postwar Central Europe.

  RELEVANCE: Directly challenges teleological periodizations (continuity or rupture models alike) that read the 1918 conversion as philosophically predetermined by earlier commitments; reframes the "young Lukács" as embedded in a generational and institutional milieu rather than a self-contained philosophical trajectory.

