  },
  keywords = {Frankfurt-School, Feenberg, reification, philosophy-of-praxis, High},
  abstract = {This chapter discusses the many uses and abuses of the Lukácsian theory and compares them with the actual content of Lukács’s early Marxist work. It presents the core of Lukács’s argument in something like its original meaning and considers its significance for the Frankfurt School, which drew on Lukács’s theory of reification despite strong reservations. The author outlines his own understanding of Lukács’s theory, and then indicates some of the various ways in which Adorno, Marcuse, and Jürgen Habermas take up its themes. He considers the critiques of Lukács in Adorno, Habermas, and Axel Honneth, and explains his disagreements with their attempts to come to terms with this influential and inconvenient predecessor. In conclusion, the chapter returns to some suggestive hints in Honneth and Marcuse that could form the basis for further development of Lukács’s concept of resistance.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{devyver2025freedomdialectics,
  author = {Devyver, Anke},
  title = {Freedom and Dialectics: On the Critical Theory of Moishe Postone and Theodor Adorno},
  journal = {Philosophy and Social Criticism},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {51},
  issue = {1},
  pages = {50--76},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Devyver examines the relationship between Postone's critique of "traditional Marxism" and Adorno's negative dialectics, arguing that despite Postone's explicit distancing from certain Frankfurt School positions, his account of abstract domination and the possibility of freedom under capitalism can be productively read alongside--and is arguably indebted to--Adorno's dialectic of enlightenment and critique of identity thinking.

  RELEVANCE: Connects the domain's two major later-reception strands (Frankfurt School negative dialectics and Postone's critique of traditional Marxism), showing that Postone's rejection of Lukácsian labor-centered critique does not amount to a simple return to, or departure from, Adornian critical theory but a distinct third position shaped by both.

  POSITION: Comparative reconstruction situating Postone's critique of traditional Marxism (including Lukács) within, rather than simply against, the Frankfurt School's negative-dialectical tradition.
  },
  keywords = {Postone, Adorno, traditional-Marxism, dialectics, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{schubertova2025radicalcontinuity,
  author = {Schubertová, Anna},
  title = {On Radical Continuity: Realism and Utopia in Lukács and Jameson},
  journal = {Thesis Eleven},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {193},
  issue = {1},
  pages = {107--123},
  doi = {10.1177/07255136251357314},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Schubertová traces four successive reconfigurations of the realism/utopia relationship across Lukács's career (from the negative utopianism of Theory of the Novel to the "immanent utopianism" of his later realism theory) and Jameson's parallel development, arguing that Jameson's late theory of utopian representation is best understood as a gradual emancipation from the Lukácsian model of representation that nonetheless preserves a deeper methodological continuity with it.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses how Jameson reconstructs (rather than simply applies or abandons) Lukács's realism/utopia framework over a full career, providing fine-grained periodization useful for the domain's Jameson-Lukács reception strand.

  POSITION: Continuity thesis: Jameson's apparent departure from Lukácsian representation theory is read as an internal development of, rather than a break from, the Lukácsian project.
  },
  keywords = {Jameson, realism, utopia, Lukács, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper explores the evolving relationship between utopia and realism in the works of György Lukács and Fredric Jameson, centering on four subsequent reconfigurations of these concepts in their writing. With respect to Lukács, the focus is on his transition from negative utopianism in his early theory of the novel to writing later about “immanent utopianism” in his theory of realism and the historical novel. Regarding Jameson, the article follows the development of his writing on utopia. Jameson's thinking about utopian form reflects his development from the Lukácsian model of representation to an alternative model in which the utopia takes center stage. I argue that Jameson's late theory of utopian representation, applied to utopia as well as contemporary historical novels, can be seen both as a gradual emancipation from Lukács’ framework and an attempt at a more radical methodological continuity in a changing historical situation.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{homer1998fredricjameson,
  author = {Homer, Sean},
  title = {Fredric Jameson: Marxism, Hermeneutics, Postmodernism},
  year = {1998},
  publisher = {Polity},
  address = {Cambridge},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Homer's book-length study traces Jameson's intellectual development from his early work introducing Western Marxist dialectical criticism (Marxism and Form) through his theorization of postmodernism, showing how the Lukácsian categories of totality, reification, and realism are continuously reworked across Jameson's career in dialogue with structuralism, poststructuralism, and Lacanian psychoanalysis.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the fullest synoptic account of how Lukácsian categories are transmitted through, and transformed within, Jameson's oeuvre, making it a key secondary source for tracing the Lukács-Jameson line of reception across four decades.

  POSITION: Comprehensive critical exposition of Jameson's Marxist hermeneutics, with sustained attention to its Lukácsian and Western Marxist roots.
  },
  keywords = {Jameson, totality, Western-Marxism, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}

@article{devlin2021korsch,
  author = {Devlin, Nicholas},
  title = {Karl Korsch and Marxism's Interwar Moment, 1917--1933},
  journal = {History of European Ideas},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {48},
  issue = {5},
  pages = {574--593},
  doi = {10.1080/01916599.2021.1975149},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Devlin situates Korsch's Marxism and Philosophy and subsequent work within the specific interwar political and intellectual context (the German revolution's failure, debates within Comintern Marxism) that shaped both its historicist argument and its reception, correcting decontextualized readings of Korsch as a purely philosophical interlocutor of Lukács.

  RELEVANCE: Supplies the historical specificity needed to compare Korsch's and Lukács's parallel 1923 interventions on their own terms rather than as interchangeable instances of "Western Marxism," sharpening the domain's comparative account of the movement's founders.

  POSITION: Historicist recontextualization of Korsch's Marxism and Philosophy within its immediate interwar political moment.
  },
  keywords = {Korsch, Western-Marxism, interwar, historicism, Medium},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT This article offers a major reinterpretation of the nature of interwar Marxist theory. It does so by offering a new reading of the work of Karl Korsch in the context of a network of ex-communist intellectuals. In Marxism and Philosophy (1923), Korsch responded to the split in the labour movement with a radical new claim to Marxist orthodoxy. Rather than engaging in Marx exegesis, he aimed to turn the Marxist ‘method’ on Marxism's own history. In the narrative he constructed, Bolshevik-inspired Communism appeared as the next logical step in the dialectic. But this argument rested on a historicisation of Marx's own writing that led to an unresolvable tension in Korsch's work that threatened to undermine its claim to Marxist orthodoxy. Once it is understood that Marxism was a political currency as much as a purely theoretical space of argument, Korsch's reluctance to resolve the tension one way or the other becomes understandable. This reinterpretation of Korsch's work challenges the ‘Western Marxism’ paradigm in which he has been read, showing that Korsch’s work presupposed a reading of revolutionary success and potential rather than defeat and that he did not advocate a turn to superstructural or cultural questions in the manner supposed.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{skidmorehess2022onehundredyears,
  author = {Skidmore-Hess, Daniel},
  title = {One Hundred Years of the Dialectic: Reflections on "Marxism and Philosophy"},
  journal = {Class, Race and Corporate Power},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {10},
  issue = {1},
  doi = {10.25148/crcp.10.1.010583},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: On the occasion of its centenary, Skidmore-Hess reassesses Korsch's Marxism and Philosophy, arguing that Korsch's historicist thesis--that Marxist theory is itself a moment of the class struggle rather than a neutral science--anticipated debates about the sociology of knowledge and theory-practice unity that remain unresolved in later Western Marxism, including in Lukács's parallel project.

  RELEVANCE: A recent, centenary-occasioned reassessment that keeps Korsch's specific contribution visible alongside the more heavily discussed Lukácsian version of the same 1923 historicist turn, useful for the domain's comparative Western Marxism section.

  POSITION: Centenary reassessment affirming the continuing relevance of Korsch's historicist thesis about Marxist theory's own class character.
  },
  keywords = {Korsch, historicism, Western-Marxism, Medium},
  abstract = {In a recent article appearing in Spectre Journal, Darren Roso concludes his discussion of “Weimar’s Marxist Heretic: Reading Karl Korsch Today” by stating that “It is, perhaps, a good but dark time to read authors like Korsch who articulated something timeless about revolutionary Marxism.” What Roso finds “timeless” is the importance of “timeliness” in “absolute” commitment to human liberation. One might hesitate before a timeless absolute, yet it is noteworthy that the text that is most central to the intellectual legacy of Karl Korsch (1886-1961) has now reached its centenary and Roso may well be correct to claim that is indeed a good, albeit dark, time to re-read Korsch. This essay will not retread the ground covered by Roso, the reader is strongly encouraged to learn more about the life and work of Karl Korsch by accessing Roso’s timely and excellent contribution. This essay will instead focus on “Marxism and Philosophy,” and consider to what extent it remains timely for our times, mostly but not necessarily only for those who share ongoing progressive and socialist commitments.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{lotz2021goldmannredivivus,
  author = {Lotz, Christian},
  title = {Lucien Goldmann Redivivus},
  journal = {Metodo: International Studies in Phenomenology and Philosophy},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {9},
  issue = {2},
  pages = {241--272},
  doi = {10.19079/metodo.9.2.241},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lotz reassesses Goldmann's Lukács and Heidegger, arguing that a proper approach to the Heidegger-Lukács relationship should be grounded in their respective appropriations of the concept of categories, and that Goldmann's genetic-structuralist framework, though influential, both illuminates and distorts the philosophical stakes of Lukács's and Heidegger's shared diagnosis of reified/inauthentic modern existence.

  RELEVANCE: A contemporary critical reassessment of Goldmann's influential but controversial thesis about Lukács's and Heidegger's convergence, situating Goldmann's own appropriation of Lukács within present-day phenomenological and Marxist scholarship.

  POSITION: Critical retrieval of Goldmann's Lukács-Heidegger thesis, correcting its categorial framework while preserving its comparative ambition.
  },
  keywords = {Goldmann, Heidegger, Lukács, categories, Medium},
  abstract = {In this paper, I first argue that Lucien Goldmann’s attempt to confront Heidegger and Lukács, though it deserves to be revitalized, remains ultimately insuficient. Second, I propose that a more fruitful reading of Heidegger and Lukács should be based on three aspects: first, Lukács’ concept of social totality should be related to Heidegger’s concept of world. Second, the more meaningful way to confront Lukács and Heidegger on the level of praxis is not the everyday dealings with ready-to-hand things (as Goldmann argues), but, instead, the second division of Being and Time in which a political concept of subjectivity and action comes to the forefront. Third, a proper approach to Heidegger’s and Lukács’ philosophies should be based on their respective appropriation of the concept of categories.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{jay2020postoneabstraction,
  author = {Jay, Martin},
  title = {Moishe Postone and the Vicissitudes of Abstraction},
  journal = {Critical Historical Studies},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {7},
  issue = {1},
  pages = {3--8},
  doi = {10.1086/708115},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: In this memorial reflection, Jay reconstructs the core argument of Postone's Time, Labor, and Social Domination--that "traditional Marxism," including Lukács's, wrongly criticizes capitalism from the standpoint of concrete labor and production rather than recognizing that the duality of concrete/abstract labor and use-value/value is itself a function of the capitalist mode of production--and reflects on Postone's proposed antidotes to abstract domination.

  RELEVANCE: A concise, authoritative restatement of Postone's critique of Lukácsian/traditional Marxism by a leading historian of Western Marxism and the Frankfurt School (author of Marxism and Totality), useful for framing Postone's place within the wider tradition this domain surveys.

  POSITION: Sympathetic restatement and appreciation of Postone's critique of traditional (including Lukácsian) Marxism's standpoint of labor.
  },
  keywords = {Postone, abstraction, traditional-Marxism, Jay, Medium},
  abstract = {T he only task more difficult than writing a talk on the central role of abstraction in Moishe Postone’s remarkable oeuvre is trying to condense its argument into a mere 20 minutes, producing, as it were, an abstract of its essential points. My sole consolation is that any attempt to do so performatively exemplifies the impoverishment of human life caused by the process of abstraction that Moishe so powerfully lamented. A great deal has been written about his critique of that process—indeed, the literature on his work is no less robust today than it was when his magnum opus, Time, Labor, and Social Domination, was published a quarter century ago—and I do not want to squander my limited time doing what Moishe himself was so often forced to do: painstakingly rehearse the main argument of that book. Instead, I want to focus on the ways in which he conceptualized the antidote to what he saw as the twin tyrannies of abstract labor and abstract time and then finish with a few remarks defending the virtues of a certain version of abstraction, which I think Moishe would have shared. Still, a few quick points do have to bemade about his larger argument for those in the audience who do not have it at their fingertips. Moishe challenged what he disparagingly called “traditional Marxism” by rejecting the idea that a critique of capitalism can be made from the point of view of a transhistorical or ontological notion of unalienated labor, labor that is concrete rather than abstract. Nor can it be criticized from the point of view of production per se as opposed to inequitable distribution. Instead, it requires understanding that the duality of concrete and abstract labor is a function of the capitalist mode of production itself, which also generates the contrasting categories of value and wealth. Value is the objectification or reification of abstracted labor, in which the qualitative specificity of producing objects for use is transformed into the quantitative fungibility of commodified labor power},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

